Z||000003||WKDFIRO||07-06-2000||08:33 PM||||Hmmmmm...I guess I'll be the first one to reply...<P>A huge percentage of cars on the road are transverse engined. Compare the Fiero to the new Honda V6 in terms of what you have to take off just to look at the plug wires on that. They (being V6s) are no harder to work on than any other car on the road. If anything they are easier. At least for me. But I used to work at a shop that did nothing BUT Fieros so I am biased.<P>Next....<P>Being most cars are front-wheel drive the propensity (big word!) for wheel slip for them are more than a Fiero. When a car accelerates the weight transfers to the back. In a front wheel drive car the weight has lessened on the wheels propelling. If not for traction control the bigger engines would spin much more than they do now. Fieros have the engine near the wheels in the back. Weight that has transfered to the back is now on the drive wheels. A mid-engine advantage. Disadvantage, mid engine cars (Ferrari included) are much more likely to understeer (front wheels plow forward instead of turning the car) than front wheel drive cars. Manufacturers tend toward safer understeer than the opposite, over steer (when the back end comes around to visit the front.) Midengine understeer is easily compensated for with more aggressive suspension tuning. BOTTOMLINE:<P>We drive circles around Hondas.<P>Oh and no, they don't burst into flames when hit from behind.<BR> <BR>My Fiero 101 lesson is now dismissed.<BR>Have fun!<P>(wow I guess I took to long typing to be the first to comment! lol)<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 07-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||07-06-2000||08:34 PM||||Terry, all fieros have bird dodo on the seats<BR>because of the sun roof  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> lol.<P>I didn't start this rumor!||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||07-06-2000||08:45 PM||||oh no!!!!!!! is their a recall for that??? bird doo doo? and I thought these were rare, one-of-a-kind seatcovers -- better get the sunroof shut before i install Mr. Mike's leather!!<P>Seriously ArchMagi, I have driven a GT for the past 8 years and I have no problem stopping (in rain OR snow -- just like any other car, keep the brakes and tires in good shape and don't drive too fast for the road conditions. As for the hard to work on because of space, I remember in 1965 I bought a brand new Pontiac GTO -- couldn't get an oil filter wrench on the oil filter to change it it was so cramped for space..........none of them have a lot of room (like an old 50's Ford flat-head or a '29 model A) any more.....the challenge is part of the fun!!! -- now, can anybody tell me whether the power steering fluid reservoir is located on the right side or the left side of the engine? -- DXR_DAD?||||reg||1
Z||000006||wkayl||07-07-2000||12:27 AM||||The power steering reservoir is located on the right.  Anyhow that is the hand I grab the can cooler with.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cupholder_2.jpg"> <BR>BTW that is Diet Coke in there.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||07-07-2000||12:30 AM||||Arch, with a forum like this we FieroNs have a easy job working on or finding info on these little toys. Every car has it's problems, but someone here has had the same problem with their fiero and can tell you what's what and how to fix it. All the little tricks of the trade are here.<BR>THE FIERO IS NOT A BAD CAR!!<P><BR>DXR_DAD ||||reg||14
Z||000008||tgowens||07-07-2000||01:56 AM||||wkayl, that looks more like a Heiniken keg can in there!!! -- that is kinda a nifty place to hang a drink holder too - right in front of the air conditioning vents to keep it cold!! I'm curious, what is holding it on, velcro maybe??<P>Arch, DAD is right -- while we sometimes get a little silly, it is all in fun but -- there is a LOT of good info here and well over 1,000 forum members who, not only share in the love of the Fiero but, have had their share of problems with them........any of us will be glad to help at any time.......maybe we have had the problem and fixed it successfully or know of someone who has.....don't worry about rumors.......besides, they said rock 'n' roll would never last in the 50's and it survives even today..........by the way, you didn't tell us which year and model Fiero you have.......<P>------------------<BR>My '87 Fiero GT -  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero/</A><BR> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
