Z||000010||dogtired||07-07-2000||05:45 AM||||Personally!<BR>I do not think they are Rummors.<BR>I think they are TRUE.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>1st off They are hard to work on. But I challange anyone that says that to show me something worth having that isn't. Have you ever tried to put a starter on an older vette? They are hard to get under, you have to jack them way up in the air??? Have you ever tried to put plugs in a Lincoln Cont. with a 460? Or a 70s T Bird, man there isn't room enough to cuss down beside the motor and fender well??? Actually it is a relative comment that certain cars are hard to work on. If all you have ever computed on is a computer with a MicroSoft operating system them you go to a machine with OS2 Lunix or a Mac operating system you will find it hard to work on for awhile till you learn that system???<P>Now as far as handling! Lets take Grandmas old Granada with an Automatic tranny 4cyl motor and never drive it over 30MPH with brand new original equipment tires, it will be smooth sailing. But lets take the same car amd put a V8 and 4 speed with relative large slick tires and wet roads and it will not be that stable either???? Back in the 70's I had a full sized chevrolet with 8inch slicks on it. My daily driver  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> anyway many rainy day on the straight and level I would do 180s real easy.<P>Taking your Fiero somewhere to have someone work on it will usually be expensieve because mechanics are there to make money the fastest way they can. And a Fiero, if serviced properly will take more time than the clone cars they see 5 or 6 of a day so the Fiero will not let that mechanic make a quick buck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> Therefore you are told they are hard to work on and will usually try and charge you more.<P>I am nervous letting anyone not familar with a Fiero drive my cars. The center of gravity is in a different place than clone cars. therefore it handles differently Mid engine vehicles take some getting used to, ask anyone who ever drove a corvair, VW or exotic sports cars. Have you ever driven a go-Kart? I only ask that as a demonstration of my point in handling differences. <P>One more RUMOR you didn't ask about is that: THEY ALL CATCH ON FIRE!<BR>Now there is a true RUMOR.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> If it were true how could there be so many still on the roads today?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> It is true there was a manufacturing error that caused some fire problems mostly in 84 but, proper user maintenance, and assuring the Recalls have been completed will lessen the potential hazzard. Back in the 50's 60's and early 70's you saw many cars of all brands with big burnt spots in the middle of the hood. This was caused by a backfire thru the carb and the unused gas would catch fire. So would it have been fair to say all carburated motors are a Fire Hazzard?? I think NOT!<P>OK I will get off my soap box. My father was probably the best at comming up with excueses why I shouldn't purchase or have anything he didn't want me to have. One could debate either side of these issues and still belive they were correct reguardless of which position they took. There are good cases either way.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>These are just FUN cars and being just a little different doesn't necessarily make them hard to work on or slippery.<P>Just what I THINK!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000011||DJRice||07-07-2000||09:16 AM||||Hehe.....<P>I would tend to agree with most of the opinons expressed so far.  <P>Is a Fiero any <B><I>harder</B></I> to work on than any other transversly mounted engine/transaxle?<P>No.  However, kiss the skin on your knuckles goodbye.<P>As for sliding, etc.  If your brakes and tires are in good shape, you should have no problem, unless you <B><I>want</I></B> to slide around.<P>Fieros, like most other automobiles, will be good to you, if you are good to them.<P>Can I get a witness???||||reg||1
Z||000012||lennyb4||07-07-2000||09:36 AM||||Amen brother rice..<BR>1.)As for being hard to work on. We have a 90 Berretta and a 70 MG. I would rather work on my fiero anyday of the week before the other two. Try changing the coils or module on a 4cyl berretta one day.<BR>2.)Also i do alot of highway driving and i've never had my fiero hydroplane or spin on water yet.<BR>3.) The braking system on fieros although not all it could be it is still better them most (4 wheel disc -vs- front disk & rear drum. Ive never had a problem stopping in a emergency.<P>If you own one now then welcome, if not then i (and everyone else here) says toi hell with the non-believers and sceptics. Buy one (or two..or three  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) and you will never regret it.||||reg||6
